{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N7sPh532T2AWZLBcJiE48o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's a divergence in `--files0-from=-` output. Let me decode:\n\ngwc output (converting from od -c):\n```\n1 1 6 a.txt\n2 3 14 b.txt\n3 4 20 total\n```\n\nGo wc output:\n```\n 1  1  6 a.txt\n 2  3 14 b.txt\n 3  4 20 total\n```\n\nThe difference is in column width. gwc uses width 1 for the first columns (1, 1, 2, 3), but Go uses width 2 (with leading spaces).\n\n\nThe Go implementation pre-stats all files to determine column widths, so when reading from stdin with `--files0-from=-`, it computes the maximum width needed across all files and the total line. Since the byte count reaches 20, it allocates width 2 for all numeric columns. gwc appears to use a different approach, possibly formatting columns as it processes each file rather than pre-computing widths. Looking at the byte-level output more carefully, I'm noticing the column widths aren't consistent across rows — the numbers have varying widths but aren't being padded uniformly. Let me trace through the actual positions to understand what gwc is doing with the formatting, especially since it's reading filenames from stdin first before processing them. I'm seeing the difference now—the Go implementation is using a field width of 2 characters for each column, which adds extra spacing, while gwc uses width 1 for the first two columns, resulting in more compact output.\n\nBut wait, when I tested with `--files0-from=filelist.txt` reading from an actual file, the outputs matched perfectly. The inconsistency only appears when the file list comes from stdin with `--files0-from=-`. This suggests GNU wc might be computing column widths differently depending on whether it's reading the file list from stdin versus a regular file.\n\nLet me double-check my earlier test to make sure I'm comparing the same setup correctly. I'm realizing the key difference